meta:
  origin:
    # Data product / Snapshot
    title: How a century of ammonia synthesis changed the world
    description: |-
      Erisman et al. (2008) trace the impact of the Haber-Bosch process, which converts atmospheric nitrogen into ammonia, on world food production. Figure 1 of the paper shows the trend in world population along with an estimate of the share of the world population that is sustained by synthetic nitrogen fertilizers produced via the Haber-Bosch process. The authors estimate that by 2000 nitrogen fertilizers were responsible for feeding 44% of the world population, rising to 48% by 2008.

      This snapshot contains that share of the world population fed by synthetic nitrogen fertilizers, as read off Figure 1 by Our World in Data in 2017.
    date_published: "2008-09-28"

    # Citation
    producer: Erisman et al.
    citation_full: |-
      Erisman, J. W., Sutton, M. A., Galloway, J., Klimont, Z., & Winiwarter, W. (2008). How a century of ammonia synthesis changed the world. Nature Geoscience, 1(10), 636-639. https://doi.org/10.1038/ngeo325

    # Files
    url_main: https://www.nature.com/articles/ngeo325
    date_accessed: '2017-10-24'

